## Writing Code That Works With Any Type

**Generics** let you write functions and types that work with **any** type, not just one specific type.

### Generic Functions
```xiom
fn first[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> T {
  return items[0];
}
```

T is a **type parameter** — a placeholder for any type.

```xiom
let nums = Vec[Int]();
nums.push(1); nums.push(2);
let n = first[Int](&nums);  // T = Int

let words = Vec[Str]();
words.push("hello");
let w = first[Str](&words);  // T = Str
```

Note: XIOM uses **square brackets** for type parameters.

### Multiple Type Parameters
```xiom
fn pair[T, U](a: T, b: U) -> (T, U) {
  return (a, b);
}
```

### Constrained Generics
```xiom
fn max[T: Ord](a: T, b: T) -> T {
  if a > b { return a; }
  return b;
}
```

T: Ord means T must support ordering.

### Why Generics?
- **No code duplication** — write one function instead of many
- **Type safety** — the compiler knows first[Int] returns an Int
- **Performance** — generics are monomorphized at compile time, zero runtime overhead

